var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-path-wicca"]=(()=>{var n=Object.defineProperty;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var d=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var l=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var c=(a,e)=>{for(var o in e)n(a,o,{get:e[o],enumerable:!0})},u=(a,e,o,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let t of d(e))!l.call(a,t)&&t!==o&&n(a,t,{get:()=>e[t],enumerable:!(i=h(e,t))||i.enumerable});return a};var y=a=>u(n({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),a);var w={};c(w,{journey:()=>s});var r="Never leave a lit candle unattended. Use a sturdy holder on a heatproof surface, away from curtains, hair, loose sleeves, pets and children, and snuff it rather than blowing it out.",m="Burn incense in a heatproof dish with sand or salt under it, in a ventilated room. Skip smoke if you have asthma, are pregnant and sensitive to it, or keep birds or other small pets nearby; a bowl of fresh herbs or a sprig of rosemary works instead.",f="Keep offerings and feast food away from pets: chocolate, grapes and raisins, onion and garlic, xylitol and alcohol are dangerous to dogs and cats, and lilies (flowers, leaves and pollen, even the water in the vase) can kill a cat. Check guests for allergies before sharing cakes, and use juice instead of wine if you or anyone present does not drink.",s={id:"path-wicca",kind:"path",paths:["wicca"],title:"Wicca: A Year and a Day",summary:"A solitary year of study and practice in modern Wicca, from its real history to the circle, the moons and the sabbats, ending in a self-dedication to the Goddess and the God.",welcome:`This is a year and a day of learning and practising Wicca on your own, at your own pace. You will read about where Wicca came from, keep the full moons and the eight sabbats, build an altar, start a Book of Shadows, and at the end decide whether to dedicate yourself to the path.

It is a self-dedication, not an initiation. Traditional Wicca, the Gardnerian and Alexandrian lines and others descended from them, is a mystery religion: its initiations are given in person by an initiated priestess or priest of a coven, its rites are oathbound, and nothing in an app can replace that. What this journey offers is the long-standing open practice of the solitary: study for a year and a day, then make your own promise. If you later seek a coven, everything you learn here will still serve you.

Nothing here is ever failed. If you miss a moon, the next one comes. Pause whenever you need to.`,about:"Wicca is a modern Pagan religion that took public shape in England in the late 1940s and 1950s, chiefly through Gerald Gardner and the coven around him, and was then reshaped by Doreen Valiente and many others. It honours a Goddess and a God, often seen in the moon and the sun, the earth and the wild, and keeps eight seasonal festivals (sabbats) and the full moons (esbats) in a cast circle with the four quarters called. Gardner claimed it was the survival of an ancient witch religion; historians, above all Ronald Hutton, have shown that it is a new religion built in the twentieth century from older pieces: ceremonial magic, Freemasonry, folk custom, Romantic poetry and a now-discredited theory of a pagan witch-cult. That does not make it less real. Wicca today runs from small oathbound covens tracing lineage back to Gardner, through teaching traditions like Reclaiming, to millions of eclectic and solitary practitioners worldwide.",notes:["This is a self-dedication. Initiation into Gardnerian, Alexandrian and other traditional Wicca is oathbound and given in person by a coven; this journey does not confer it, and nobody who has only done this journey should call themselves an initiate of those traditions.","No coven ritual text appears here. The Wiccan Rede, the Charge of the Goddess and coven Books of Shadows are described in our own words only.","Wicca is a modern religion with old roots. This journey says plainly which parts are old and which are twentieth-century; you can love the practice and still be honest about its history.","Traditional Wicca has been built around male and female polarity. Many covens and most solitaries today practise inclusively, and the app does too: you are welcome here whatever your gender or sexuality.","Fire, smoke, knives and food all appear in Wiccan practice. Every rite carries safety notes; read them.","A year of inner work can stir up old grief or low moods. That is common and nothing to be ashamed of. If it becomes heavy, pause the journey and talk to someone you trust, your doctor or a counsellor; in a crisis, call your local emergency number or a helpline such as Samaritans (116 123 in the UK and Ireland) or 988 in the US.","White sage is not used in this journey. Its wild harvest and its place in closed Indigenous practice make it a poor fit; rosemary, juniper, bay, mugwort and frankincense are used in its place (skip mugwort if you are pregnant)."],holyDays:["samhain","yule","imbolc","ostara","beltane","litha","lammas","mabon"],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"Crossing the threshold",intro:"Two weeks to begin: a first lesson on what Wicca is and what this year will ask of you, a simple daily devotion, a fresh notebook that will become your Book of Shadows, and a short rite to say out loud that your year and a day has started.",days:14,lessons:[{id:"wicca-what-it-is",title:"What Wicca is, and what this year is",summary:"A plain map of the religion, the difference between dedication and initiation, and how the year will run.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"A religion, not only a craft",body:`Wicca is a religion. It has gods, a sacred calendar, a shape of ritual and a set of values, and people practise it the way others keep any faith: at an altar at home, on particular nights, with a sense of being in relationship with something larger. It also includes magic, the craft part of witchcraft, but the heart of it is worship and the turning of the seasons.

Most Wiccans honour a Goddess and a God. The Goddess is often seen in the moon, the earth and the sea; the God in the sun, the grain and the wild animal, sometimes shown with horns. They meet at the full moons, called esbats, and at eight seasonal festivals, the sabbats, which together make up the Wheel of the Year. Rites usually happen inside a cast circle, with the four directions called at its edge.`},{heading:"Dedication is not initiation",body:`The Wicca that Gerald Gardner made public in the 1950s is an initiatory mystery tradition. You join it when an initiated priestess or priest of a coven brings you in, in person, after a period of getting to know each other. Its rites and its Book of Shadows are oathbound: initiates promise not to share them. Gardnerian and Alexandrian Wicca still work this way, as do several smaller lines descended from them. In Britain and America they are often grouped together as British Traditional Wicca.

From the 1970s onward, published books taught Wicca to people with no coven nearby, and the solitary practitioner became the most common kind of Wiccan in the world. Solitaries usually mark their start with a self-dedication: a promise made by you, to the gods and to yourself. Doreen Valiente, who had been Gardner's high priestess, published a self-initiation rite in 1978, and Scott Cunningham's 1988 guide for solitaries made the idea mainstream. A self-dedication is real and meaningful. It is not a coven initiation, does not give you a lineage, and does not make you Gardnerian or Alexandrian. This journey keeps that line clear.`},{heading:"Why a year and a day",body:`Many teachers ask newcomers to study for a year and a day before dedicating or seeking initiation. The phrase is old: in medieval English and Welsh law a year and a day was the span after which a claim lapsed or a status became fixed, and it turns up in ballads and fairy tales as the length of a spell or a stay in the otherworld. Modern Wicca borrowed it because it fits. In a year and a day you meet every sabbat once and about thirteen full moons, and you find out whether the practice still fits you in the dark of winter as well as at the bright of May.

This journey follows that rhythm. The study stage runs through roughly the first half of the year, a lesson every couple of weeks, between one moon and the next. The practice stage carries you through the rest. Then comes a few weeks of honest discernment and, if you choose it, the dedication.`},{heading:"What you need",body:`Very little. A notebook you like, which becomes your Book of Shadows. A small surface for an altar: a shelf, a windowsill, the top of a dresser. A white candle, or an LED one if open flame is not allowed where you live. A bowl for water and a pinch of salt. Everything else, the knife, the cup, the wand, can come later or never.

You also need a little time: ten minutes a day for devotion, an hour or so at each full moon, a longer evening at each sabbat. If you have less, do less. A practice kept small and steady will carry you further than a grand one you abandon in March.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Wicca as a named religion is documented from the early 1950s in Gardner's writing and the records of his covens. Initiatory covens with oathbound rites have existed continuously since then. Self-dedication and self-initiation rites were published by Valiente (1978) and spread widely through solitary guides in the 1980s.",unknown:'How many Wiccans there are is uncertain: censuses in England and Wales, the US and Australia catch some, but many solitaries never tick the box. The legal and folklore uses of "a year and a day" are well attested; who first made it the standard period of Wiccan study is not.'},inPractice:"Choose a start date, ideally near a new or full moon. Clear a small surface for an altar, put a candle and a bowl of water on it, and write on the first page of your notebook the date, the moon's phase and one honest sentence about why you are here.",reflect:["What first drew you to Wicca, and what do you hope it will give you?","How do you feel about being a solitary for now? Is a coven something you want one day?","What would make this year feel like a success, even if you decide at the end not to dedicate?"],further:["Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (1999; 2nd ed. 2019): the standard history.","Thea Sabin, Wicca for Beginners (2006): a clear, friendly overview of practice.","Scott Cunningham, Wicca: A Guide for the Solitary Practitioner (1988)."],library:["traditional/tool/book_of_shadows","traditional/tool/altar"],premade:["wicca-daily-devotion"]}],practices:[{id:"wicca-threshold-devotion",title:"A daily devotion",instructions:"Each day, at the same time if you can, light your candle, greet the Goddess and the God in a sentence of your own, sit quietly for a few minutes, and snuff the candle. Morning or evening both work. If a day slips, simply begin again the next.",times:14,every:"day",prompt:"What did you notice in the quiet today, in your body, the weather, your mood?",premade:"wicca-daily-devotion"},{id:"wicca-threshold-book",title:"Begin your Book of Shadows",instructions:"Set up your notebook: a title page with your name and start date, a few blank pages at the front for an index, and a section at the back for a moon log. From now on, write after every rite, however short.",times:1,premade:"kitchen-solitary-book-of-shadows-blessing"}],rite:{title:"Lighting the first candle",summary:"A short opening rite: cleanse the space, light a candle, and tell the Goddess and the God that you have begun a year and a day of learning.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"A white candle in a safe holder",ref:"traditional/candle/white"},{name:"A small bowl of water",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A pinch of salt"},{name:"A sprig of rosemary, fresh or dried",ref:"traditional/herb/rosemary",optional:!0},{name:"Your new notebook and a pen",ref:"traditional/tool/book_of_shadows"}],preparation:"Choose an evening near the new or full moon. Tidy and dust your altar space. Wash your hands and face, put your phone in another room, and decide in advance one sentence you want to say about why you are beginning.",steps:[{title:"Settle",instructions:"Stand or sit before the altar. Breathe slowly until your shoulders drop. Feel your feet on the floor and the weight of your body.",minutes:2},{title:"Cleanse with salt and water",instructions:"Drop the salt into the water and stir it sunwise (clockwise) with a finger. Sprinkle a few drops around the altar, and touch a drop to your forehead. If you have rosemary, dip it and use it to sprinkle.",spoken:"Salt and water, earth and sea, make this place clean and make me ready."},{title:"Light the candle",instructions:"Light the candle and look at the flame for a moment.",spoken:"Lady of the moon, Lord of the sun and the green wood: I have come to learn your ways.",light:!0},{title:"Say why you are here",instructions:"Say your sentence out loud, then add that you will study for a year and a day before you decide anything more. Speak plainly; nobody is grading you.",spoken:"For a year and a day I will watch the moon and keep the turning of the year, and listen, and learn. At the end I will choose freely."},{title:"Sit and listen",instructions:"Sit quietly with the candle for a few minutes. Notice whatever comes, or nothing at all.",minutes:5,timed:!0},{title:"Write the first entry",instructions:"In your notebook write the date, the moon's phase, what you said and anything you noticed. This is page one.",minutes:5},{title:"Close",instructions:"Thank the Goddess and God, snuff the candle, and pour the water outside on soil or down the sink with thanks.",spoken:"Thank you. I will come back.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Eat something and drink a glass of water. Tomorrow, begin the daily devotion.",safety:r}},{kind:"study",title:"Study: history, gods and the shape of the rite",intro:"About six months, one lesson every two weeks or so, read between one full moon and the next. You will learn where Wicca really came from, who its gods are, how a circle is cast and why, what the tools are for, and how the moons and the sabbats are kept. Keep every esbat and sabbat that falls in this time, even simply.",days:170,lessons:[{id:"wicca-gardner",title:"Gerald Gardner and the birth of Wicca",summary:"Who Gardner was, what he claimed, and how Wicca went public in the 1950s.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"A retired civil servant in the New Forest",body:`Gerald Brosseau Gardner (1884 to 1964) spent most of his working life in Ceylon, Borneo and Malaya as a rubber planter and customs official, and came home to England in 1936 with a lifelong interest in folklore, weapons, naturism and the occult. He settled at Highcliffe, on the edge of the New Forest on the south coast, joined a local esoteric group, the Rosicrucian Order Crotona Fellowship, which ran a small theatre in Christchurch, and through people he met there was, he said, initiated in September 1939 into a coven of witches whose craft had come down through families.

Gardner told versions of that story for the rest of his life, though the details shifted between tellings. He named a local woman, "Old Dorothy" Clutterbuck, as one of the circle; for years sceptics doubted she had existed, until Doreen Valiente tracked down her birth and death records in the 1980s. Historians have tested the rest hard. Some of the people he mentioned were real and did know him; whether a working coven with an ancient lineage existed is another matter, and the evidence for it is thin. Ronald Hutton concluded that something may have existed in the New Forest, but that the religion Gardner taught owed far more to his own reading and creativity than to any surviving witch-cult.`},{heading:"Going public",body:`Until 1951 the Witchcraft Act of 1735, which made pretending to witchcraft or fortune-telling a crime, was still law in Britain. In 1949 Gardner published High Magic's Aid, a novel with witchcraft rites in it. After the Act was replaced by the Fraudulent Mediums Act in 1951, he went further: Witchcraft Today (1954) and The Meaning of Witchcraft (1959) presented witchcraft as a living, ancient, nature-loving religion, and he spoke to newspapers and on television. He ran a museum of witchcraft at Castletown on the Isle of Man and initiated a stream of new people into covens, notably at Bricket Wood near St Albans.

Gardner called the people of the religion "the Wica". The name Wicca for the religion itself spread in the 1960s and 1970s. The word comes from Old English wicca, a male sorcerer, with wicce as its female form; the modern pronunciation with a hard "k" is a modern convention, since the Old English was said closer to "witcha".`},{heading:"What Gardner put together",body:`Look at Gardner's early rites and you can see where the pieces came from. The magic circle, the consecrated tools and the four quarters came from ceremonial magic, especially the Renaissance grimoire known as the Key of Solomon and the late Victorian Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn. Degrees of initiation and some of the language of oaths echo Freemasonry, which Gardner had joined. Some early passages were adapted from Aleister Crowley, whom Gardner met in 1947. The idea of a horned god and a moon goddess drew on Margaret Murray's theory of a surviving witch-cult, on Charles Leland's book Aradia (1899) about Italian witches, and on a century of English poetry in love with Pan and the greenwood.

None of this is a scandal. Every religion is built from what came before it. The honest picture is that Gardner, with a handful of collaborators, wove these threads into something new and coherent, and that people found in it a living religion.`},{heading:"Why the history matters for practice",body:`Knowing where things came from changes how you hold them. When you cast a circle, you are working in a magical form with a recorded history going back to Renaissance grimoires, which is plenty old. When you keep the full moon, you are joining a custom that Wiccans have kept since the 1950s, not one unbroken since the Stone Age. You can practise wholeheartedly and still say "this is modern" without flinching.

It also protects you. People who believe Wicca is a direct survival of an ancient religion are easy to mislead with fake histories, invented "ancient" texts and false claims of lineage. People who know the real history can enjoy the craft's romance without being taken in by it.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Gardner's biography, his books, his involvement with the Crotona Fellowship, his meeting with Crowley in 1947, the repeal of the 1735 Act in 1951, and the existence of his covens from the early 1950s are all well documented, notably in Philip Heselton's biographies and Hutton's history.",unknown:"Whether a coven initiated Gardner in 1939, and what it did, cannot be confirmed; the only account of the initiation is Gardner's own. Heselton argues for a real New Forest group drawn from the Crotona circle; Hutton and others think it was at most a small, recent circle, not an ancient survival. No document of any witch religion before Gardner's has been found."},inPractice:"At your next altar time, light your candle for the people who built this practice, whatever you think of their claims: Gardner, the coven members whose names we know, and the unnamed folk practitioners whose charms and customs fed it.",reflect:["Does it change anything for you that Wicca is modern? Why or why not?","Which of the threads Gardner wove together interests you most: the ceremonial magic, the folklore, the poetry?"],further:["Philip Heselton, Witchfather: A Life of Gerald Gardner (2012).","Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon, chapters on Gardner.","Gerald Gardner, Witchcraft Today (1954): read it as a primary source, knowing its history claims are not reliable."]},{id:"wicca-murray-hutton",title:"The witch-cult that never was",summary:"Margaret Murray's theory, why historians abandoned it, and what Hutton's Triumph of the Moon showed instead.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Murray's theory",body:`Margaret Murray (1863 to 1963) was an Egyptologist at University College London. In The Witch-Cult in Western Europe (1921) and The God of the Witches (1931) she argued that the people tried as witches in early modern Europe were members of a real pagan religion, a fertility cult older than Christianity, organised in covens of thirteen, worshipping a horned god, meeting at quarterly festivals, and surviving in secret for centuries. She wrote the entry on witchcraft for the Encyclopaedia Britannica in 1929, and it stayed there for decades, so her theory became what most educated people believed.

Murray is where Wicca got several words. "Esbat" for an ordinary coven meeting comes from her reading of French trial records. "Coven" was a rare Scottish word she made common. Her horned god, her calendar of four great festivals and her picture of a hidden religion shaped Gardner's whole story.`},{heading:"Why it fell apart",body:`From the 1960s historians went back to the trial records Murray had used and found she had quoted selectively, leaving out the fantastical parts (flying, shape-changing, the Devil as a literal being) that sat alongside the parts she liked. The trials themselves turned out to be mostly about neighbourly accusations of harmful magic, often extracted under pressure or torture, and the elaborate "sabbat" was largely a fantasy of learned demonologists. Norman Cohn's Europe's Inner Demons (1975) and Keith Thomas's Religion and the Decline of Magic (1971) were turning points. No serious historian now accepts that the accused were members of a pagan religion.

That does not mean there was no magic in old Europe. There were cunning folk, charmers and healers selling remedies and protection, and plenty of seasonal customs with obscure roots. But those people were, as far as we can tell, Christian in their own eyes, and they did not form a hidden church of the old gods.`},{heading:"The Triumph of the Moon",body:`In 1999 Ronald Hutton, a historian at the University of Bristol, published The Triumph of the Moon: A History of Modern Pagan Witchcraft. Instead of asking whether Wicca was ancient, he traced where each of its elements actually came from: the Romantic poets' worship of nature and the goddess, the Victorian revival of Pan, ceremonial magic, Freemasonry, folklorists like James Frazer, the woodcraft movement, Murray, Leland, Robert Graves's The White Goddess (1948), and finally Gardner and his circle.

His conclusion was that Wicca is a new religion, perhaps the only one England has given the wider world, and a genuine, creative product of modern culture rather than an old survival. Many Wiccans were hurt by the book at first. Over time most came to value it. It let people stop defending a story that could not be defended and start taking pride in what their religion actually is.`},{heading:"Holding it honestly",body:`You will still meet the old story in books, especially ones published before 2000 and some written since: the Burning Times with nine million dead witches, an unbroken goddess religion from the Stone Age, a coven tradition handed down in secret. The figure of nine million goes back to a German writer's rough extrapolation in 1784 and was spread by the American feminist Matilda Joslyn Gage in 1893; it has no basis in the records. Careful modern estimates for the European witch trials are around 40,000 to 60,000 executions between about 1450 and 1750, roughly three-quarters of them women, nearly all of them Christians accused by neighbours. That is horror enough and deserves remembrance on its own terms.

You can still feel kinship with those who were persecuted, and with the folk healers and charmers of the past. Just call it kinship, not descent.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Murray's books, her Britannica entry, and the scholarly refutation by Cohn, Thomas and others are firmly established. Modern estimates of witch-trial executions, drawn from surviving court records, cluster around 40,000 to 60,000.",unknown:"Records of many trials are lost, so totals remain estimates, and scholars give slightly different ranges (Brian Levack suggests about 45,000; others go a little higher or lower). The share of women also varied sharply by region: in Iceland and parts of Russia most of the accused were men. How much genuine pre-Christian belief survived in folk custom (as distinct from a cult) is debated and varies by place; Carlo Ginzburg's work on the Friulian benandanti shows some striking popular beliefs, but not a pagan church."},inPractice:"Go through any Wicca book on your shelf and pencil a small mark in the margin wherever it makes a historical claim. As this journey goes on, check a few of them. Keep the ones that hold.",reflect:["Have you believed parts of the old story? How does it feel to set them down?","What could remembering the victims of the witch trials look like in your practice, honestly done?"],further:["Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (1999; 2nd ed. 2019).","Ronald Hutton, The Witch: A History of Fear (2017).","Norman Cohn, Europe's Inner Demons (1975)."]},{id:"wicca-traditions-today",title:"Valiente, the traditions, and eclectic Wicca",summary:"Doreen Valiente's legacy, the main traditions, and the difference between traditional and eclectic Wicca.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Doreen Valiente",body:`Doreen Valiente (1922 to 1999) was initiated by Gardner at midsummer 1953 and became high priestess of his Bricket Wood coven. She noticed how much of his Book of Shadows was borrowed from Crowley and others, and rewrote much of it in her own poetry and prose, including the best-loved liturgy of the religion. She broke with Gardner in 1957 over his publicity seeking and a set of coven rules he produced, and went on to work with other groups.

Later she wrote books that opened the craft to people outside covens. Witchcraft for Tomorrow (1978) included a rite for self-initiation, an extraordinary step from someone who had held the heart of the initiatory tradition. Her The Rebirth of Witchcraft (1989) is a frank, warm insider's history, and well worth reading.`},{heading:"The traditions",body:`Gardnerian Wicca descends from Gardner's covens by initiation. It has three degrees, works in covens led by a high priestess and high priest, and keeps its rites and Book of Shadows oathbound. Traditionally it works skyclad, that is, naked in the circle.

Alexandrian Wicca was founded in the 1960s by Alex and Maxine Sanders in London. It is close to Gardnerian Wicca in structure, with more ceremonial magic and often robes. The two are frequently grouped as British Traditional Wicca, and many covens today hold both lineages.

Other traditions grew from the same root. Raymond Buckland brought Gardnerian Wicca to the United States in 1964 and in 1973 founded Seax-Wica, which allowed self-initiation. Starhawk's The Spiral Dance (1979) gave rise to the Reclaiming tradition, which joins witchcraft with activism and works in open, non-hierarchical circles. Dianic Wicca, associated with Zsuzsanna Budapest from the 1970s, centres the Goddess and women's mysteries; some Dianic groups exclude trans women, which many other Wiccans, and this app, do not support.`},{heading:'Traditional, eclectic and "reconstructionist"',body:`Most Wiccans today are eclectic: they learned from books, classes and the internet, practise alone or in informal groups, and build a practice from many sources around a recognisably Wiccan core. Traditional, initiatory Wiccans sometimes say that without the oathbound material and initiation this is not Wicca at all but something else, perhaps "witchcraft" or "Neo-Wicca". Eclectics often reply that a religion belongs to the people who live it. Both views are sincerely held, and you will meet both. The polite course is simple: call yourself a solitary or eclectic Wiccan, never claim a lineage you do not have, and respect a coven's right to keep its secrets.

You may also hear Wicca contrasted with reconstructionist paganism. Reconstructionists, such as many Heathens and Hellenic polytheists, try to rebuild the religion of a particular ancient people as closely as the evidence allows. Wicca does not aim to do that; it is a revival in the broader sense, drawing on many sources and openly modern in its structure. Some people move from one to the other, or practise both side by side. If you find yourself drawn to one pantheon and wanting to know exactly how the ancients honoured it, a reconstructionist path may suit you better, and that is a good discovery.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Valiente's initiation, her rewriting of the Book of Shadows, her 1957 split and her published self-initiation rite are documented in her own books and in Hutton. The founding dates and leaders of the Alexandrian, Seax, Reclaiming and Dianic traditions are well recorded.",unknown:`Exactly which passages of the Gardnerian Book of Shadows are Valiente's and which are Gardner's or borrowed is partly known and partly argued over, and initiates cannot confirm details publicly. Where the line falls between "Wicca" and "witchcraft" is a matter of opinion, not fact.`},inPractice:"In your Book of Shadows, write a short honest description of your own practice as it stands: solitary, eclectic, drawn to which gods, influenced by which books. You will revise it as the year goes on.",reflect:["Do you feel drawn toward a traditional coven one day, an eclectic path, or a reconstructionist one?","How do you want to describe yourself to other Pagans, and why?"],further:["Doreen Valiente, The Rebirth of Witchcraft (1989).","Thorn Mooney, Traditional Wicca: A Seeker's Guide (2018).","Starhawk, The Spiral Dance (1979)."]},{id:"wicca-goddess-god",title:"The Goddess and the God",summary:"Who Wiccans worship, how they understand their gods, and the names and forms you will meet.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Two who are many",body:`At its core Wicca honours a Goddess and a God, often called the Lady and the Lord. The Goddess is linked with the moon, the earth, the sea, birth and the cycles of life; the God with the sun, the grain that is cut and grows again, the horned beasts of the forest and the realm of death and rebirth. They are partners, and the turning of the year is often told as their story: the God born at Yule, growing through spring, joined with the Goddess at Beltane, giving his life with the harvest, and returning.

Wiccans understand this pair in different ways. Some see them as two real deities. Some see all goddesses as faces of one Goddess and all gods as faces of one God, a view rooted in the occultist Dion Fortune's line that all the gods are one god and all the goddesses one goddess. Some see a single divine source behind both. Some treat them as powerful symbols of nature and the psyche. You do not have to decide this now. Notice which one rings true as you practise.`},{heading:"The Horned God",body:`The Wiccan God is often shown horned or antlered. Murray took the horned devil of the witch trials as a disguised pagan god; that theory failed, but the image stuck. Wiccans connect the God with Cernunnos, the antlered figure whose name is known chiefly from one first-century Gaulish carving, the Pillar of the Boatmen in Paris; with Pan, the goat-footed Greek god whom Victorian and Edwardian writers made a symbol of wild nature; and with Herne the Hunter, a ghost of Windsor Forest first mentioned in Shakespeare's Merry Wives of Windsor. These are real sources of very different kinds: one a Gaulish god about whom almost nothing is known, one a well-documented Greek deity, one a piece of English folklore.

He is also the Green Man, the leafy face carved in medieval churches (a decorative motif whose pagan meaning is a modern reading), and the sun king who dies and returns.`},{heading:"The Goddess, and the triple goddess",body:`The Goddess is often called by many names: Diana and Aradia from Leland's Italian material, Cerridwen, Brigid, Isis, Selene, Hekate and others. Many Wiccans see her as Maiden, Mother and Crone, matching the waxing, full and waning moon. That triple form is modern: it comes mainly from Robert Graves's The White Goddess (1948), a poet's brilliant and unreliable book. Ancient religion did have triple goddesses, such as the Irish Morr\xEDgna and the Roman Matres, and Hekate was shown with three bodies, but none of them meant maiden, mother and crone. It is a meaningful modern symbol, and you can use it as one.

Traditional covens know their gods by particular names that are oathbound. Solitaries can work with the Lady and Lord unnamed, choose a pair from a single pantheon, or follow where a particular deity seems to call. If you find one god or goddess taking over your practice, the app's deity journeys are there for you.`},{heading:"Polarity and inclusion",body:"Traditional Wicca puts weight on the polarity of female and male, Goddess and God, priestess and priest; the energy between them powers the rites. That can be beautiful, and it has also left people out: same-sex couples, trans and non-binary people, anyone who does not fit the pairing. Since the 1990s many covens have rethought this, seeing polarity in many pairs (light and dark, giving and receiving, still and moving) rather than in bodies. Yvonne Aburrow and others have written about inclusive Wicca. Here, every gender is welcome at the altar, and you may honour the Goddess, the God, both, or one more than the other."}],knowAndDont:{known:"The Pillar of the Boatmen (Paris, first century CE) carries the main inscription naming Cernunnos; antlered figures appear elsewhere, such as on the Gundestrup cauldron, without a name. Herne first appears in Shakespeare (c. 1597). Graves's White Goddess (1948) is the source of the Maiden-Mother-Crone scheme. Wicca's duotheism is documented from Gardner onward.",unknown:"What Cernunnos meant to the Gauls is almost entirely unknown. Whether the gods are real persons, faces of one power or symbols is a theological question no evidence settles; Wiccans genuinely disagree."},inPractice:"Place two candles on your altar, one silver or white for the Goddess and one gold or yellow for the God. Light both at your daily devotion for a week, and greet each by whatever name feels right, or simply as Lady and Lord.",reflect:["How do you sense the divine right now: as two, as many, as one, as nature itself?","Is there a particular goddess or god already standing at the edge of your practice?"],further:["Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon, chapters on the Goddess and the Horned God.","Yvonne Aburrow, All Acts of Love and Pleasure: Inclusive Wicca (2014).","Charles Godfrey Leland, Aradia, or the Gospel of the Witches (1899; public domain)."],library:["traditional/deity/cernunnos","traditional/deity/diana","traditional/deity/cerridwen","traditional/candle/silver","traditional/candle/gold"],premade:["wicca-daily-devotion"]},{id:"wicca-circle",title:"The circle and the quarters",summary:"How a Wiccan rite is shaped, where the form comes from, and how to cast and close a circle.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"Why a circle",body:`Almost every Wiccan rite takes place inside a cast circle. It does two jobs. It marks out a space set apart from ordinary life, a "place between the worlds" as Wiccans often say, and it holds in the power raised inside until it is released. Older ceremonial magicians drew circles for protection, to keep spirits out; Wicca kept the protective idea and added the sense of a temple you make wherever you need one.

You walk the circle deosil, sunwise, which in the northern hemisphere means clockwise, the way the sun's shadow moves. Moving the other way, widdershins, is traditionally for undoing and is used when a circle is taken down. In the southern hemisphere the sun moves the other way across the northern sky, and some practitioners there cast anticlockwise; others keep clockwise as a convention. Either is defensible.`},{heading:"The four quarters",body:`At the circle's edge Wiccans call the four quarters: east, south, west and north, each tied to an element. The usual modern pattern, inherited from the Golden Dawn, is east for air (dawn, breath, thought), south for fire (noon, will, passion), west for water (sunset, feeling, the sea) and north for earth (midnight, body, the land). The elements themselves come from Greek natural philosophy, from Empedocles in the fifth century BCE. Some traditions call the quarters as Watchtowers or as Guardians; some call elemental spirits; some call the winds. Many add spirit at the centre.

The correspondences are a convention, not a law. If you live with the ocean to your east, you might well put water there. In the southern hemisphere many people move fire to the north, toward the sun. What matters is that the map makes sense to you and that you use it consistently.`},{heading:"The shape of a rite",body:`Wiccan rites vary, but most public ones follow the same order. First you prepare: bathe, set out the altar, sweep the space with a besom. Then you cleanse it with salt water and incense, cast the circle by walking its edge with athame, wand or finger, and call the quarters starting in the east. You invite the Goddess and the God. Then comes the heart of the rite: a seasonal observance, a meditation, magic, a reading. Then cakes and ale (or wine, or juice), blessed and shared, with a portion set aside as an offering. Then you thank the gods, release the quarters in reverse order, and take down the circle widdershins.

That order, open, work, share, close, is the backbone you will use all year. It works at a full altar or with nothing but your pointed finger in a studio flat.`},{heading:"Doing it small",body:`You do not need a large room. A circle can be as small as the space you sit in; you can trace it with your finger while seated, or visualise it growing outward from your body. If you share a home, you can cast silently. If you cannot burn incense, waft a sprig of rosemary or ring a bell. If you forget a step, nothing terrible happens; simply do it when you remember.

One rule is worth keeping firmly: if you cast a circle, take it down afterwards. Leaving things open makes for a scattered, uneasy feeling, and closing well is half of good practice.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The protective magic circle is documented in medieval and Renaissance grimoires such as the Key of Solomon; the element-direction correspondences are set out in Golden Dawn papers of the 1890s; the four classical elements go back to Empedocles. The general shape of Wiccan circle-casting appears in Gardner's and Valiente's published works.",unknown:"Coven-specific wording and details of casting are oathbound in traditional Wicca and not public. There is no single correct southern-hemisphere practice; communities in Australia, New Zealand and South Africa differ."},inPractice:"Work through the app's Casting and Closing the Circle rite three times in the next two weeks, with nothing else in the middle. Get the opening and closing into your body before you add anything inside.",reflect:["How did the space feel inside the circle compared with outside?","Which elements and directions make sense where you live? Would you change any?"],further:["Janet and Stewart Farrar, A Witches' Bible (1996; first published as Eight Sabbats for Witches, 1981, and The Witches' Way, 1984): the published form of much Alexandrian practice.","Thea Sabin, Wicca for Beginners, chapters on ritual structure."],library:["traditional/tool/athame","traditional/tool/besom","traditional/tool/wand","traditional/tool/salt_circle"],premade:["wicca-casting-the-circle","green-four-elements-balancing-rite","green-elemental-altar-setup"]},{id:"wicca-tools",title:"The tools and the altar",summary:"The traditional working tools, what each is for, how to consecrate them, and what you can do without.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The working tools",body:`Gardner's covens used a set of working tools drawn largely from the Key of Solomon. The athame is a double-edged, usually black-handled knife used to direct power and cast the circle; it is never used to cut anything physical. The white-handled knife, sometimes called a boline, is the practical one for cutting cord, herbs or carving candles. The wand is used to invite and call, the pentacle (a disc, often of wood, metal or clay, engraved with a five-pointed star in a circle) represents earth and is used to bless and ground things, the chalice holds the wine or water, and the censer holds incense. Covens also use a sword, cords, and in traditional practice a light ceremonial scourge; you will read about these in older books.

Many Wiccans link the tools to the elements: pentacle to earth, chalice to water, and athame and wand to air and fire, though traditions disagree about which of those two is which. Pick one scheme and stay with it.`},{heading:"Getting tools",body:`Old advice says never haggle over a magical tool, and that tools you make or find carry more of you. Both are good ideas, but neither is a rule. A kitchen knife you set aside and never use for food can be an athame. A plain wine glass is a chalice. A stick from a tree you love, dried and smoothed, makes a fine wand. A saucer with a star drawn on it is a pentacle. You can build a full altar for very little money.

A note on knives: in many countries, including the UK, carrying a bladed knife in public without good reason is an offence, and a ritual purpose may not count. Keep your athame at home and transport it wrapped and out of reach, and check the law where you live before bringing one to a public ritual. Keep blades away from children.`},{heading:"Setting up the altar",body:`There is no single correct altar layout, but a common one puts Goddess symbols (a silver candle, a shell, the chalice) on the left and God symbols (a gold candle, an antler or acorn, the athame) on the right, with the pentacle in the middle and the censer near the front. Many people face the altar north, to earth, or east, to the rising sun. A cloth, a seasonal decoration and a small offering dish complete it.

Keep it clean. Dust gathers on a forgotten altar the way silence gathers in a forgotten friendship, and tending it is itself a devotion.`},{heading:"Consecrating a tool",body:`Before a tool is used in the circle, Wiccans consecrate it: cleanse it of other uses, then dedicate it to its purpose. A simple form is to pass the tool through the four elements (a sprinkle of salt for earth, the smoke of incense for air, a careful pass above a candle flame for fire, a touch of water) and then to hold it and state what it is for and which gods it serves. Many people then use it at once, so its first act is a sacred one.

After that, treat it with care. Keep it on the altar or wrapped in cloth, don't lend it casually, and clean it after use.`}],knowAndDont:{known:`The athame, white-handled knife, wand and pentacle all appear in versions of the Key of Solomon; the athame's name has been traced to "arthame" in some French manuscripts of that grimoire. Gardner describes the tools in Witchcraft Today and High Magic's Aid.`,unknown:"The origin of the word athame beyond those manuscripts is uncertain. Elemental assignments for the athame and wand differ between traditions, and no early source settles them."},inPractice:"Choose one tool this month, most people start with a chalice or a wand, find or make it, and consecrate it with the app's rite at the next full or waxing moon.",reflect:["Which tool are you most drawn to, and why?","What object already in your home could become sacred with intention?"],further:["Janet and Stewart Farrar, The Witches' Way (1984), on the tools in Alexandrian practice."],library:["traditional/tool/athame","traditional/tool/boline","traditional/tool/wand","traditional/tool/pentacle","traditional/tool/chalice","traditional/tool/incense_burner","traditional/tool/altar","traditional/tool/cauldron"],premade:["wicca-consecrating-a-tool"]},{id:"wicca-esbats",title:"The esbats: keeping the full moon",summary:"What an esbat is, where the word came from, and how to hold one alone.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"Meeting by the moon",body:`An esbat is any Wiccan gathering that is not a sabbat, and in practice most are held at or near the full moon. The word comes from Margaret Murray, who took it from early modern French trial records (from an old verb meaning to frolic or amuse oneself). Covens meet at the full moon because the Goddess is honoured as the moon, because magic is traditionally thought strongest then, and, practically, because before streetlights the full moon gave light to walk home by.

There are twelve or thirteen full moons a year. A second full moon in a calendar month is now popularly called a blue moon, a usage that comes from a 1946 magazine article misreading an almanac rule; it is a fine excuse for a special working all the same.`},{heading:"What happens at an esbat",body:`A solitary esbat follows the circle's familiar shape. You cast and call the quarters, invite the Goddess and God, and then honour the moon: some people stand in moonlight or face her through a window, some light a silver candle, some say words of praise they have written. Then comes any magic or divination for the month, a meditation, or simply quiet. Cakes and ale follow, with a portion set aside for the gods, and then the circle is closed.

In covens the high priestess may draw down the moon, an initiatory rite in which the Goddess is invoked into her. The name echoes ancient Greek tales of Thessalian witches who could pull the moon from the sky. It is a coven mystery, best learned there; a solitary can stand in moonlight and open to the Goddess in their own way without imitating it.`},{heading:"Moon names, and the other phases",body:`The popular full moon names (Wolf, Snow, Worm, Pink and the rest) came into wide use through American farmers' almanacs in the twentieth century. They mix colonial, English and loosely attributed Native American sources and follow northern seasons. Use them as seasonal folklore, not as an ancient calendar, and in the south feel free to shift or replace them with names that fit your own year.

Many Wiccans also mark the new moon, for beginnings, and the dark moon, for rest and inner work. You don't need to do everything. Keeping every full moon for a year is already a real practice.`},{heading:"An esbat in a small flat",body:`You don't need a garden or moonlight to keep an esbat. Check the time of moonrise in the app or a weather site: the full moon rises around sunset, and in a city you may see her from a window only late in the evening. If clouds or buildings hide her, face the direction she will be and work by candlelight. A bowl of water on the sill catches her reflection for scrying if the angle is right.

A small working esbat might take forty minutes: ten to set up and cleanse, five to cast and call, ten with the moon in praise and quiet, ten for a little magic or a tarot reading, five for cakes and ale and closing. In a shared home, you can cast and call silently and speak to the Goddess under your breath. What matters is that you show up every month.`}],knowAndDont:{known:`Murray introduced "esbat" in 1921. Full-moon meetings are documented in Gardner's covens from the 1950s. The "second full moon in a month" meaning of blue moon traces to a 1946 Sky & Telescope article. Greek references to Thessalian witches drawing down the moon go back to Aristophanes' Clouds (423 BCE).`,unknown:"Whether the full moon actually strengthens magic is a matter of belief, not evidence. The origins of individual moon names are often unclear, and many attributions to specific Native nations are unreliable."},inPractice:"At the next full moon, hold a full esbat using the app's rite. Afterward, start a moon log at the back of your Book of Shadows: date, moon name, weather, what you did, how you felt.",reflect:["What did the moon look like, and how did it feel to stand with her?","Which phase of the moon do you feel most at home in?"],further:["Deborah Lipp, The Elements of Ritual (2003), for a step-by-step look at the parts of a rite."],library:["traditional/candle/silver","traditional/crystal/moonstone","traditional/crystal/selenite"],premade:["wicca-full-moon-esbat","wicca-cakes-and-ale","cosmic-new-moon-intentions","cosmic-dark-moon-rest-shadow","wicca-blue-moon-long-wish"]},{id:"wicca-sabbats",title:"The sabbats in Wicca",summary:"How the eight festivals entered Wicca, the story Wiccans tell through them, and how to keep them alone.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"From four to eight",body:`Gardner's early covens kept four "great sabbats", following Murray: the eves of February, May, August and November, which he called by their English church names Candlemas, May Eve, Lammas and Hallowe'en. Around 1958 his Bricket Wood coven added the two solstices and two equinoxes, partly through the influence of his friend Ross Nichols, who would later found the Order of Bards, Ovates and Druids. The eightfold Wheel of the Year was born.

The modern names came later still. Imbolc, Beltane, Lughnasadh and Samhain are Irish festival names found in medieval Irish texts. Yule is the old Germanic midwinter name. Ostara, Litha and Mabon, for the spring equinox, midsummer and autumn equinox, were chosen by the American Pagan Aidan Kelly around 1974 and spread from there. The Wheel of the Year journey goes into each festival's history in detail.`},{heading:"The story of the year",body:`Wiccans often tell the sabbats as a single story. At Yule the Goddess gives birth to the sun God. At Imbolc she recovers and the light grows. At the spring equinox the young God and Goddess meet. At Beltane they unite and the land blossoms. At midsummer the God is at his height. At Lammas he gives himself as the grain. At the autumn equinox he goes into the dark. At Samhain the veil is thin, the dead are honoured, and the God rules the underworld until his rebirth. Some Wiccans also tell of the Oak King and the Holly King, twin brothers who fight at the solstices for the waxing and waning year, an idea that grew out of Frazer and Graves and was popularised by Janet and Stewart Farrar in Eight Sabbats for Witches (1981).

These stories are modern liturgy, and many Wiccans love them. Others focus more on what the land is doing where they live. Both are proper ways to keep the sabbats.`},{heading:"Keeping a sabbat alone",body:`A solitary sabbat uses the same frame as an esbat: cast, call, invite, observe, share, close. What changes is the middle. Decorate the altar with what the season offers. Do something the season asks for: light a candle at Yule, make a Brigid's bed at Imbolc, plant seeds in spring, gather herbs at midsummer, bake bread at Lammas, give thanks at the autumn equinox, set a place for the dead at Samhain. Then feast, even if the feast is a slice of good bread and a glass of juice.

Go outside if you can. A sabbat is about the season, and the season is outdoors.`},{heading:"Sabbats and esbats together",body:"Over a year you will keep around thirteen esbats and eight sabbats. The sabbats are the year's great festivals, about the sun and the land; the esbats are the monthly heartbeat, about the moon and your own working. Some people simply call the sabbats festivals and the esbats ordinary meetings. Getting both into your calendar is the single most useful thing you can do this year. The app has rites ready for each."}],knowAndDont:{known:"Gardner's four-festival calendar and the 1958 adoption of the solstices and equinoxes at Bricket Wood are recorded by Hutton. The Irish names are attested in medieval Irish literature. Aidan Kelly has written that he coined Ostara, Litha and Mabon as sabbat names around 1974.",unknown:"Who exactly proposed adding the solar festivals in 1958 is not fully clear. The God-and-Goddess sabbat cycle and the Oak and Holly King are modern; there is no evidence that any ancient people told the year in this form."},inPractice:"Put all eight sabbats and the next thirteen full moons in your calendar now, with a reminder a week before each sabbat to plan it.",reflect:["Which sabbat are you most looking forward to, and which feels least like yours?","Does the story of the God and Goddess through the year move you, or do you relate more to the land and weather?"],further:["Janet and Stewart Farrar, Eight Sabbats for Witches (1981).","Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (1996), on the real history of the British ritual year."],premade:["wicca-samhain-dumb-supper","wicca-yule-log-sun-vigil","wicca-imbolc-brigids-bed","wicca-ostara-seed-and-egg","wicca-beltane-flower-crown","wicca-litha-noon-herb-gathering","wicca-lammas-bread-offering","wicca-mabon-thanks-and-balance"]}],practices:[{id:"wicca-study-esbat",title:"Keep the full moon",instructions:"At each full moon, hold an esbat: the full rite when you have the time, or at minimum light a candle, go to a window or outside, and greet the moon. Read the next lesson in the days after.",times:7,every:"esbat",prompt:"Moon log: the moon's name, the weather, what you did, and one thing you felt or noticed.",premade:"wicca-full-moon-esbat"},{id:"wicca-study-sabbat",title:"Keep the sabbat",instructions:"At each sabbat that falls in this stage, hold its rite from the app or make your own, using the frame of the circle. Decorate your altar for the season and eat something seasonal.",times:4,every:"sabbat",prompt:"What is the land doing right now where you live? How did the rite match it, or not?"},{id:"wicca-study-devotion",title:"Weekly altar time",instructions:"Once a week, clean and refresh your altar, change the water, light your candles, and spend ten quiet minutes there. Keep the daily devotion if you can; this is the floor beneath it.",times:24,every:"week",prompt:"What changed this week on your altar and in you?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Practice: ethics, magic and the solitary life",intro:"Roughly the second half of your year. The lessons turn from history and form to how you live: Wiccan ethics, magic, the Book of Shadows, solitary practice and how to find community safely. Keep every moon and sabbat; by the end of this stage you will have kept the whole Wheel once.",days:154,lessons:[{id:"wicca-ethics",title:"Wiccan ethics",summary:"The Rede's idea, the threefold return, and how Wiccans actually make moral choices.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The Rede in plain words",body:`The best-known statement of Wiccan ethics is the Wiccan Rede. "Rede" is an old word for counsel or advice. Its idea, put in our own words, is this: so long as you harm no one, do what you truly will. Doreen Valiente spoke a version of it in public in 1964, and a longer rhyming poem with the same ending circulated from the 1970s.

Read closely, it is permission more than prohibition. It says that the things you choose freely are yours to choose, so long as they don't cause harm. It does not say that every act that might harm anyone is forbidden, which would make it impossible to eat, drive or argue. Most Wiccans read it as a call to think hard about consequences: who could be hurt by this, including me, and is it worth it?`},{heading:"The threefold return",body:`Many Wiccans also hold that what you send out comes back to you threefold. The idea of magic returning multiplied appears in Gardner's 1949 novel, and from the late 1960s writers including Raymond Buckland popularised it as a "law". It is not in the older layers of the tradition as a rule, and many traditional Wiccans dislike it, seeing it as a borrowed idea of karma used to frighten beginners.

A useful way to hold it: actions have consequences, often larger and stranger than we expect, and the person most changed by your magic is you. You don't need a cosmic accountant to take that seriously.`},{heading:"Values you will meet",body:`Beyond the Rede, certain values run through Wiccan practice. Personal responsibility: there is no one to forgive you but those you harmed, and no devil to blame. Reverence for nature, since the gods are found in it, which for many Wiccans means real care for the earth in daily life. Freedom of conscience: Wiccans don't proselytise, and most hold that every person finds their own way. Honesty and keeping your word, especially oaths. In covens, the ideal of perfect love and perfect trust among members, and the custom of never charging for initiation or teaching the craft's core.

Consent matters too. Most Wiccans think it wrong to do magic on another person without their permission, even healing, and certainly anything meant to bend someone's will, such as love spells aimed at a particular person.`},{heading:"Hard cases",body:`Ethics gets interesting where values collide. Is protection magic that sends harm back to an attacker acceptable? Many say a binding to stop harm is, a curse is not; this app keeps its workings defensive. Is it wrong to do a healing spell for your sick friend who has said nothing about wanting one? Ask them. Is eating meat a violation of "harm none"? Wiccans differ; some are vegetarian, many are not, most try to eat with gratitude and awareness.

Write your own ethic. The Rede is a starting point, not a substitute for thinking.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Valiente's 1964 speech gave the first public form of the Rede's idea; the long rhyming Rede poem was published in the 1970s. A threefold return of magic appears in Gardner's High Magic's Aid (1949), and its use as a moral law grew in the 1970s.",unknown:`Who first formulated the Rede is unclear: Valiente attributed it to Gardner, and some have linked its shape to Crowley's Law of Thelema and to an old Rabelais motto. Whether any "law of return" operates is a matter of belief.`},inPractice:'Write a single page in your Book of Shadows titled "My ethic": three to five principles you intend to live by, in your own words, and one hard case you have faced or might face. Revisit it at the discernment stage.',reflect:["Where would you draw the line in magic, and why there?","Is there a part of Wiccan ethics that you find hard, or that you disagree with?"],further:["Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon, on the Rede and threefold law.","Doreen Valiente, The Rebirth of Witchcraft (1989)."]},{id:"wicca-magic",title:"Magic in the Wiccan way",summary:"What Wiccans mean by magic, how power is raised and directed, and the timing and ethics of spellwork.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"What magic is, here",body:`In Wicca, magic is the craft of causing change through focused will, symbol and the help of the gods and the natural world. It is done inside the circle, usually after the gods have been invited, as part of a religious rite rather than apart from it. Wiccans use it for healing, protection, finding work, clarity, blessing a home, and, most of all, changing themselves.

The occult tradition behind Wicca offers several ways to think about why it works: the will shapes reality; symbols speak to the deep mind; energy can be raised and sent; the gods answer. Many modern practitioners hold a mixture, and some treat magic as a powerful psychological practice. You can practise well without settling which is true.`},{heading:"Raising and directing power",body:`The classic Wiccan picture is that power is raised in the circle, held there, and released toward its goal. Covens raise it by chanting, dancing round the circle, drumming, or breath, until it peaks and is sent out; this is called the cone of power. Gardner listed several ways of raising power, from meditation and chant to dance and cords. A solitary can raise power by chanting a short rhyme faster and faster, by breath work, by tying knots in a cord one at a time, or by focused visualisation, then releasing it with a gesture or a shout and grounding the excess by touching the floor.

Grounding matters. If you feel buzzy, light-headed or restless after magic, press your palms to the floor, eat something and drink water.`},{heading:"Timing and correspondence",body:`Wiccans often time magic by the moon: growth and attraction while it waxes, release and banishing as it wanes, beginnings at the new moon, and anything at the full. The days of the week carry planetary rulers from the old astrological tradition (Monday the moon, Tuesday Mars, Wednesday Mercury, Thursday Jupiter, Friday Venus, Saturday Saturn, Sunday the sun), and colours, herbs and stones have their own lists of associations. These correspondence tables mostly come from Renaissance magic and nineteenth-century occult books, with plenty of modern additions.

Use them as a language. They help focus intent, and they make a spell feel coherent. Don't let them become a cage: if you need help today, you don't have to wait three weeks for the right moon.`},{heading:"A simple spell, start to finish",body:`A typical candle spell in a Wiccan frame: cast the circle and call the quarters, invite the gods, state your intent in one clear sentence, carve a symbol or word into a candle with your white-handled knife, anoint it with a little oil, and hold it while you raise power. Light it, speak your intent, and let it burn while you meditate on the outcome. Thank the gods, close the circle, and then take a practical step toward your goal in ordinary life, because magic works best when it has a road to travel down.

Record it in your Book of Shadows, and come back in a month to note what happened.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The planetary days and most traditional correspondences are documented in Renaissance and later occult sources such as Agrippa's Three Books of Occult Philosophy (1533). The cone of power and Gardner's ways of raising power appear in Gardnerian material that has been published and discussed by Hutton and others.",unknown:"There is no scientific evidence that magic causes outside change. Many modern correspondence lists combine old sources with recent invention without saying which is which."},inPractice:"Choose one honest, harmless goal for yourself, such as clarity, courage or calm, and do a single candle spell for it at the right moon phase inside a full circle. Log the result after a month.",reflect:["What do you think magic is? Has that changed this year?","How did your body feel when you raised and released power?"],further:["Scott Cunningham, Earth Power (1983).","Henry Cornelius Agrippa, Three Books of Occult Philosophy (1533; public domain translation 1651)."],library:["traditional/candle/_guide","traditional/tool/boline","traditional/tool/cauldron"],premade:["cosmic-waxing-moon-growth-jar","cosmic-waning-moon-release","folk-nine-knots","cosmic-wednesday-mercury-study"]},{id:"wicca-book-of-shadows",title:"The Book of Shadows",summary:"The history of the Book of Shadows, what coven books contain, and how to keep your own.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"Gardner's book",body:`The Book of Shadows is the witch's book of rites, lore and spells. Gardner's earliest surviving book, which he titled Ye Bok of ye Art Magical, dates from the late 1940s and holds early versions of rites, some borrowed from Crowley and the Key of Solomon. By the early 1950s he was calling it the Book of Shadows, and it was this book that Valiente rewrote. The name may have come from a 1949 article in an occult magazine about an Indian palmistry text with the same title; Gardner never explained it.

In Gardnerian and Alexandrian Wicca, the Book of Shadows is copied by hand by each new initiate from their initiator's book, and its core is oathbound. Over the years parts have been published, by the Farrars with Valiente's help and by others without permission, so you will see versions in print and online. Their accuracy varies, and traditional initiates do not confirm them. This journey does not reproduce any of it.`},{heading:"Your own book",body:`For a solitary, the Book of Shadows is your own. It is part sacred text, part lab notebook and part diary. People commonly keep: their rites as they actually perform them, invocations they have written, correspondence lists they trust, recipes for incense and oils, a moon log, sabbat notes, dreams, divination readings, and records of every spell with its result.

Some people keep two books: a rough working journal and a fair copy, written slowly and beautifully, of what has proven itself. Others keep a binder they can rearrange. Digital is fine. What matters is that you write it yourself, and that it reflects what you do rather than what you think you ought to do.`},{heading:"Writing well in it",body:`Write after every rite, even a short one, while it is fresh: date, moon, what you did, what you felt, what happened afterwards. Be honest when nothing happened. Over a year the patterns will surprise you. Mark sources: if you copy a correspondence list, note the book; if you wrote a chant, say so. That habit will save you from passing on someone else's work as tradition.

Treat the book with respect. Keep it on or near the altar, bless it at the start, and don't leave it lying about for idle reading if privacy matters to you.`},{heading:"What to put in this year",body:`For this journey, a useful structure is a section for each of the eight sabbats, with what you did and what the land was doing; a moon log with a line or two for each esbat; a section of rites you have written; a section of correspondences you have tested; and a spell record with dates and results. Add a page for each god or goddess you come to know, with what you have learned and what you have offered.

At the end of the year, these pages are the material of your discernment. A full, honest book is the best evidence you will have of whether this path is yours.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Ye Bok of ye Art Magical survives and has been studied; the Gardnerian Book of Shadows's development through Valiente's rewrites is documented by Hutton and by Aidan Kelly's Crafting the Art of Magic (1991). Hand-copying from initiator to initiate is a stated coven custom.",unknown:`The source of the name "Book of Shadows" is not certain. Published "Gardnerian" texts differ from one another, and which is closest to any coven's book cannot be confirmed by outsiders.`},inPractice:"Make a fair-copy section in your Book of Shadows and write out, slowly, the circle casting you actually use now, after months of practice, in your own words.",reflect:["Looking back over your book so far, what patterns do you see?","What would you want someone to find in your Book of Shadows a hundred years from now?"],library:["traditional/tool/book_of_shadows"],premade:["kitchen-solitary-book-of-shadows-blessing"]},{id:"wicca-solitary-coven",title:"Solitary practice today, and finding a coven safely",summary:"How solitaries keep a strong practice, how to find community, and the warning signs of a bad group.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The solitary path",body:`Most Wiccans now practise alone, at least for long stretches. Solitary practice has real strengths: you set the pace, you follow your own gods, and you can keep the moons and sabbats wherever you are. Its weaknesses are just as real. Nobody notices when you stop, nobody corrects mistakes, and it is easy to drift into reading about Wicca rather than doing it.

Good solitaries build structure. A daily devotion, however short. A fixed night for altar care. Rites written down and repeated so they deepen. A reading plan. And contact with other Pagans now and then, even if only online, for conversation and a reality check.`},{heading:"Finding people",body:`If you want company, start with open events. In the UK, the Pagan Federation (founded in 1971) lists local moots, informal meetings in pubs and caf\xE9s, and runs conferences. In North America, many cities have Pagan Pride Day events in autumn, and organisations such as the Covenant of the Goddess (1975) can point you to groups. Open circles, often run by Reclaiming communities, Unitarian Universalist pagan groups or local shops, let you attend a public sabbat. Many covens run outer courts or training circles for seekers, a way to get to know each other before anyone talks about initiation.

Traditional covens tend to be small, careful and slow to accept new members. Expect to spend months, sometimes a year or more, getting to know a coven before initiation, and expect to be asked why you want to join. That patience is a good sign.`},{heading:"Warning signs",body:`Most Pagan groups are kind and decent. A few are not, and the risk is real because covens are small, private and often involve nudity. A reputable coven will never: require sex or sexual contact as a condition of initiation or advancement; initiate anyone under eighteen; charge large fees for initiation or degrees (covering costs like candles or a shared meal is fine); demand that you cut off friends or family; pressure you to keep secrets about how you are treated, as opposed to keeping the rites themselves private; or claim a lineage they can't explain or that other initiates of that tradition don't recognise.

Traditional Gardnerian practice is skyclad. A good coven tells you this early, explains it, and lets you decide; you can always leave. Meet first in public places. Tell a friend where you are going. Ask other initiates of the tradition about a coven's lineage and reputation; legitimate covens are part of a network that knows itself.`},{heading:"Seeking initiation",body:"If at the end of this year you feel drawn to traditional Wicca, your solitary year is good preparation, but you will begin again as a seeker. That is how it should be. Be honest about your experience and humble about it. Coven training will teach you their own ways, and some things you have learned here will be done differently there. A year and a day as a solitary is a fine thing to bring to that door."}],knowAndDont:{known:"The Pagan Federation (as the Pagan Front, 1971) and the Covenant of the Goddess (1975) are long-standing organisations. Traditional covens commonly require seekers to be adults and to undergo a period of acquaintance or training before initiation.",unknown:"Local groups change constantly, and no directory is complete or vouches for every listing. Customs about nudity, training length and degree requirements vary between covens even within one tradition."},inPractice:"Look up one public Pagan event or moot within reach of you, in person or online, and go once, just to listen. Write afterwards about what you saw.",reflect:["Do you want a coven, a looser group, or a solitary practice with occasional company?","What would you need to feel safe in a group ritual?"],further:["Thorn Mooney, Traditional Wicca: A Seeker's Guide (2018).","Scott Cunningham, Living Wicca (1993), on long-term solitary practice."],premade:["kitchen-solitary-morning-practice","kitchen-solitary-evening-reflection","kitchen-solitary-grounding-centering"]}],practices:[{id:"wicca-practice-esbat",title:"Keep the full moon",instructions:"Keep every full moon with a full esbat. Use the app's seasonal moon rites for variety, or write your own and keep refining it in your Book of Shadows.",times:6,every:"esbat",prompt:"Moon log: what did you work on this month, and what happened since the last moon?",premade:"wicca-full-moon-esbat"},{id:"wicca-practice-sabbat",title:"Keep the sabbat",instructions:"Keep each remaining sabbat with a full rite. By the end of this stage you will have kept all eight. For at least one, write the rite entirely yourself.",times:4,every:"sabbat",prompt:"What does this festival mean to you now, compared with what the books said?"},{id:"wicca-practice-spell",title:"A monthly working",instructions:"Once a month, do a small piece of magic inside the circle for yourself or, with their permission, for someone else. Keep it honest and harmless, and log it.",times:5,every:"month",prompt:"What did you work for, how, and what happened in the month after?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"Discernment: is this your path?",intro:"Three weeks to look back over your year and a day before deciding anything. Read your Book of Shadows from the first page. Keep your devotion going, quietly. There is no right answer here: dedicating, waiting longer, seeking a coven, choosing another path or stopping altogether are all good outcomes, and the app will be glad of any of them.",days:21,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"wicca-discern-read",title:"Read your book from the start",instructions:"Over a week, read your Book of Shadows from the first page to the last. Mark what moved you, what bored you and what you stopped doing.",times:1,prompt:"Who were you when you started this book, and who are you now?"},{id:"wicca-discern-sit",title:"Sit with the question",instructions:`Every couple of days, nine times over these three weeks, light your candles and sit in silence for ten minutes with the question "Is this my path?" Don't try to answer. Write afterwards whatever is there.`,times:9,every:"day",prompt:"What came up when you sat with the question today?"}],questions:["Over the year, did you keep coming back to the altar because you wanted to, or because you felt you should?","Do the Goddess and the God feel like presences in your life, or ideas you like? Is that enough for you either way?","Which parts of Wicca do you love, and which do you put up with? Could you live with the second list for years?","Are you drawn to a particular pantheon or deity more than to the Wiccan pair? Would a deity journey or a reconstructionist path suit you better?","Do you want to seek a coven and initiation? If so, would dedicating now help or confuse that?","Has your ethic held up in real life this year? Is there anything you would need to change before making a promise?","What would it mean to decide not to dedicate, or not yet? How would that feel?"]},{kind:"dedication",title:"Dedication",intro:"If you choose to dedicate, take a week to prepare: write your words, choose a craft name if you want one, and pick the night, often a full moon or the sabbat nearest your start date. This is a self-dedication to the Goddess and the God and to the Wiccan path as you have come to know it. It is not a coven initiation, it does not make you Gardnerian, Alexandrian or a member of any lineage, and it does not replace initiation if you seek it later.",days:7,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"wicca-dedication-words",title:"Write your dedication",instructions:"Write your dedication in your own words: to whom you dedicate yourself, what you promise and for how long (many people dedicate for a year and renew), and what you ask in return. Keep it short enough to say from memory. Promise only what you can keep.",times:1,prompt:"Read your dedication aloud once. Does every word feel true?"},{id:"wicca-dedication-name",title:"Consider a craft name",instructions:"Many Wiccans take a craft name at dedication, used only in the circle or with other Pagans. It can come from nature, a quality you aspire to, or a word that arrived in meditation. Avoid taking a deity's name as your own. Not having one is fine.",times:1,prompt:"What name, if any, came to you, and what does it mean?"}],rite:{title:"Self-dedication to the Lady and the Lord",summary:"A full Wiccan-style self-dedication: purify, cast the circle, call the quarters, invite the Goddess and God, make your promise, anoint yourself, take your name if you wish, share cakes and ale, and close.",minutes:60,ingredients:[{name:"A white candle for the centre",ref:"traditional/candle/white"},{name:"A silver or white candle for the Goddess",ref:"traditional/candle/silver"},{name:"A gold or yellow candle for the God",ref:"traditional/candle/gold"},{name:"Four tea lights for the quarters, or four stones",optional:!0},{name:"A bowl of water and a dish of salt",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"Frankincense or another resin incense, or a sprig of rosemary",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense",optional:!0},{name:"A little olive oil, plain or with one drop of lavender oil per teaspoon",ref:"traditional/herb/lavender"},{name:"Your athame, wand or simply your finger",ref:"traditional/tool/athame",optional:!0},{name:"A chalice of wine, juice or water",ref:"traditional/tool/chalice"},{name:"A small cake, biscuit or piece of bread"},{name:"An offering dish",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"Your written dedication and your Book of Shadows",ref:"traditional/tool/book_of_shadows"}],preparation:"Choose your night. During the day, eat lightly and spend some time outdoors. Clean the altar and the room thoroughly and set out everything you need. Before the rite, take a slow bath or shower with a handful of salt or a few rosemary sprigs in the water, and dress in clean, simple clothes or a robe. Put your phone in another room.",steps:[{title:"Sweep and purify",instructions:"Sweep the space sunwise with a besom or your hand, brushing out the day. Bless the salt and water, mix them, and sprinkle the circle's edge. Carry incense or rosemary around it. Then touch the salt water to your brow, lips, heart, hands and feet.",spoken:"Let this place be clean. Let me come clean to it: mind, mouth, heart, hands and feet.",minutes:5},{title:"Cast the circle",instructions:"Stand in the east and walk the circle sunwise with athame, wand or finger, seeing a line of light rise into a sphere around you. Close it where you began.",spoken:"I cast this circle between the worlds, a place of meeting and of making. So it is.",minutes:3},{title:"Call the quarters",instructions:"Face each direction in turn, starting in the east, and light its tea light if you are using them. Call each element in a sentence of your own, or use the words given.",spoken:"East and air, come with breath and morning. South and fire, come with warmth and will. West and water, come with feeling and the tide. North and earth, come with stone and root. Be welcome.",light:!0},{title:"Invite the Goddess and the God",instructions:"Light the Goddess candle, then the God candle, then the centre candle from them both. Speak to them by the names you have come to use, or as Lady and Lord.",spoken:"Lady of the moon and the deep earth, Lord of the sun and the green wood, I have kept your moons and your festivals for a year and a day. I ask you to be here as I make my promise."},{title:"Speak your dedication",instructions:"Stand before the altar and say your dedication, from memory or from the page. Speak slowly. Then add the words below, which say clearly what this is.",spoken:"I make this promise of my own free will. It is a dedication, not an initiation; I claim no lineage but my own seeking. I give myself to your service and to this path, and I will keep faith with it."},{title:"Anoint",instructions:"Dip a finger in the oil and touch it to your brow, your breastbone and the inside of each wrist. If you have chosen a craft name, say it now for the first time.",spoken:"Marked by my own hand and seen by the gods. In the circle I am known as (your name)."},{title:"Listen",instructions:"Sit before the altar in silence. Let the gods answer in their own way, or not at all. Both are fine.",minutes:10,timed:!0},{title:"Cakes and ale",instructions:"Hold your hand over the chalice and the cake and bless them. Pour a little from the chalice and break a piece of the cake into the offering dish for the gods, then eat and drink the rest yourself.",spoken:"May I never hunger and never thirst. What I am given, I share."},{title:"Write it down",instructions:"In your Book of Shadows record the date, the moon, your dedication word for word, your name if any, and what you noticed.",minutes:5},{title:"Thank and release",instructions:"Thank the Goddess and the God. Release the quarters in reverse order, north, west, south, east, snuffing each light. Walk the circle widdershins to take it down, drawing its light back into your hand and then into the ground.",spoken:"Thank you, Lady and Lord. Earth, water, fire and air, go in peace. The circle is open but unbroken.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Take the offerings outside the next day and leave them on soil, or bury them (not where dogs can reach). Eat a proper meal. Many dedicants mark the anniversary each year with a renewal; the ongoing stage will remind you.",safety:`${r} ${m} Dilute essential oil well (about one drop per teaspoon of carrier oil), patch test first, keep it from your eyes, and check with your midwife in pregnancy. ${f}`}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Keeping the path",intro:"A dedication is a beginning, not a graduation. Keep the full moons and the eight sabbats, renew your dedication each year, keep writing in your Book of Shadows, and keep learning. If you seek a coven one day, go as a seeker, with your year behind you.",days:365,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"wicca-ongoing-esbat",title:"The full moons",instructions:"Keep each full moon with an esbat, as simply or fully as your life allows.",times:13,every:"esbat",prompt:"What did this moon bring?",premade:"wicca-full-moon-esbat"},{id:"wicca-ongoing-sabbat",title:"The eight sabbats",instructions:"Keep each sabbat. Try to vary them from year to year, and add something of your own land each time.",times:8,every:"sabbat",prompt:"How was this sabbat different from last year's?"},{id:"wicca-ongoing-renewal",title:"Yearly rededication",instructions:"On the anniversary of your dedication, or at the nearest sabbat, repeat the dedication rite in short form: cast, invite, reread your promise, renew or change it, and close. If your path has shifted, say so honestly.",times:1,prompt:"What have you kept, what have you let go, and what do you promise for the coming year?",premade:"wicca-self-dedication"},{id:"wicca-ongoing-study",title:"Keep learning",instructions:"Read one serious book a season, history, theology, folklore or practice, and make notes in your Book of Shadows. Visit a moot or open ritual a few times a year.",times:4,every:"month",prompt:"What did you learn this season that changed how you practise?"}]}],sources:["Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon: A History of Modern Pagan Witchcraft (Oxford University Press, 1999; 2nd ed. 2019).","Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun: A History of the Ritual Year in Britain (1996).","Philip Heselton, Witchfather: A Life of Gerald Gardner (2012).","Gerald Gardner, Witchcraft Today (1954) and The Meaning of Witchcraft (1959), as primary sources.","Doreen Valiente, Witchcraft for Tomorrow (1978) and The Rebirth of Witchcraft (1989).","Margaret Murray, The Witch-Cult in Western Europe (1921).","Norman Cohn, Europe's Inner Demons (1975); Keith Thomas, Religion and the Decline of Magic (1971).","Aidan Kelly, Crafting the Art of Magic (1991), on the development of the Gardnerian Book of Shadows; his naming of Ostara, Litha and Mabon in 1974 is told in his own later account (a 2017 blog post) and summarised by Hutton.","Robert Graves, The White Goddess (1948), as the source of the Maiden-Mother-Crone scheme.","Charles Godfrey Leland, Aradia, or the Gospel of the Witches (1899).","Thorn Mooney, Traditional Wicca: A Seeker's Guide (2018), on finding a coven.","All rite wording in this journey is original to the app. No oathbound material, Rede text or Charge text is quoted."]};return y(w);})();
